# Deploying User Data Scripts to Private EC2 Instances via a Bastion (VPC Peering)

This guide covers copying `userdata-server1.sh` and `userdata-server2.sh` onto running private instances in **VPC B**, 
reached through a bastion host in **VPC A** over a **VPC peering connection**, then running them and testing the Application Load Balancer (ALB).

## Architecture

```
Your Mac ──SSH──> Bastion (VPC A, public subnet)
                     │
                     └── VPC peering ──> Instance 1 (VPC B, private)
                                         Instance 2 (VPC B, private)
                                              ▲
                          Internet ──> ALB ───┘  (port 80)
```

## 0. Prerequisites (check once)

| Check | Where |
|---|---|
| Peering connection status is **Active** | VPC > Peering connections |
| VPC A route table has a route to VPC B's CIDR via the peering connection | VPC > Route tables |
| VPC B route table has a route to VPC A's CIDR via the peering connection | VPC > Route tables |
| Instance SG allows **SSH (22)** from the bastion's private IP or VPC A's CIDR | EC2 > Security groups |
| Instance SG allows **HTTP (80)** (and 8080 for Jenkins) from the ALB's security group | EC2 > Security groups |
| VPC B private subnets have outbound internet (NAT gateway) for `dnf` and the Jenkins repo | VPC > Route tables |

## 1. Set up your SSH config (on your Mac)

Edit `~/.ssh/config`:

```
Host bastion
    HostName <bastion-public-ip>
    User ec2-user
    IdentityFile /Users/<you>/Downloads/demo-project.pem

Host vpc-b-instance-1
    HostName <instance-1-private-ip>
    User ec2-user
    IdentityFile /Users/<you>/Downloads/demo-project.pem
    ProxyJump bastion

Host vpc-b-instance-2
    HostName <instance-2-private-ip>
    User ec2-user
    IdentityFile /Users/<you>/Downloads/demo-project.pem
    ProxyJump bastion
```

Make sure the key file is protected:

```bash
chmod 400 /Users/<you>/Downloads/demo-project.pem
```

## 2. Copy the scripts to the instances (from your Mac)

Run these from the folder containing the scripts. **Note the `:~/` at the end**; without the colon, scp just makes a local file.

```bash
scp userdata-server1.sh vpc-b-instance-1:~/
scp userdata-server2.sh vpc-b-instance-2:~/
```

## 3. SSH into each instance

```bash
ssh vpc-b-instance-1
```

Confirm the file arrived and is complete (about 50 lines):

```bash
ls -l ~
wc -l ~/userdata-server1.sh
```

If an old or wrong copy is lying around, remove it (this is permanent):

```bash
rm ~/<old-file-name>.sh
```

## 4. Run the script as root

```bash
sudo bash ~/userdata-server1.sh
```

It takes a few minutes. To keep it running if your SSH session drops:

```bash
sudo nohup bash ~/userdata-server1.sh > /dev/null 2>&1 &
sudo tail -f /var/log/user-data.log      # Ctrl+C to stop watching
```

> Always use `sudo`. Without root, `dnf`, `systemctl` and writes to `/etc/yum.repos.d/` fail with "Access denied" / "Permission denied".

## 5. Verify on the instance

```bash
curl -s localhost | grep Hello           # Hello from SERVER 1
systemctl is-active httpd jenkins        # active  active
sudo tail -n 5 /var/log/user-data.log    # Jenkins initial admin password
```

Then `exit` and repeat steps 3 to 5 for instance 2 using `userdata-server2.sh` (it should print **SERVER 2**).

## 6. Test the load balancer

1. In **EC2 > Target Groups**, wait until both targets show **healthy** (about a minute after Apache starts).
2. Copy the ALB's **DNS name** (EC2 > Load Balancers) and open it with `http://` in several browsers or incognito windows:

   ```
   http://<alb-dns-name>
   ```

   You will see the blue **SERVER 1** page or the green **SERVER 2** page.

3. For a clearer view of the round-robin, use curl from your Mac, since browsers often reuse one connection:

   ```bash
   for i in {1..10}; do curl -s http://<alb-dns-name> | grep Hello; done
   ```

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Likely cause |
|---|---|
| `Access denied` / `Permission denied` in script output | Script run without `sudo` |
| `here-document ... delimited by end-of-file` | Script file truncated or edited; copy it again and check `wc -l` |
| `Unit httpd.service not found` | Script never completed; re-run as root |
| `dnf` or `curl pkg.jenkins.io` hangs | No NAT gateway / outbound route from the private subnet |
| ALB **502 Bad Gateway** | Apache not running, or target group port/protocol is not HTTP:80 |
| ALB **503** | No healthy targets (check SGs and health check settings) |
| ALB times out | ALB is internal, in non-public subnets, or its SG blocks port 80 |
| Can't SSH to private instance | Peering routes missing, or instance SG not allowing 22 from the bastion |

## Optional: expose Jenkins through the ALB

Add a second listener on **HTTP:8080** forwarding to a target group on port **8080** (health check path `/login`), 
and allow 8080 on the ALB and instance security groups. Browse to `http://<alb-dns-name>:8080`. Each instance runs its own independent Jenkins, 
so logins and jobs are not shared between them.
